<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gnaeus Marcius Coriolanus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Coriolanus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gnaeus Marcius Coriolanus was a legendary Roman hero of the early 5th century BCE, said to have earned his name by capturing the Volscian town of Corioli. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, he was exiled after opposing grain distributions to the plebs, led a Volscian army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and turned back only at the pleas of his mother and wife. His story inspired Shakespeare's play Coriolanus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gnaeus-marcius-coriolanus.jpg" alt="Gnaeus Marcius Coriolanus" width="320" height="560" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Battista Tiepolo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coriolanus_at_the_Walls_of_Rome_Hermitage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
